---
import BlogLayout from '../../layouts/BlogLayout.astro';

const bodyContent = `
<p><a href="https://ai.meta.com/muse/">Meta Muse</a> runs each person's agent on a dedicated virtual machine in the cloud, and Meta says that nothing Muse does reaches the internet unless Muse's Sentinel agent approves it (<a href="https://about.fb.com/news/2026/09/introducing-muse-personal-ai-agent/">Meta's announcement</a>). Meta does not document a shell in that VM, a folder for your own skills, or how its traffic leaves the VM; its help center says Muse's skills are developed by Meta and built in (<a href="https://www.meta.com/help/artificial-intelligence/2797651547267109/">How Muse works with skills</a>). This guide is for the case where your agent can run shell commands in a VM like that and its only way out is an HTTPS proxy set in <code>HTTPS_PROXY</code>. One command installs the Pilot Protocol skills into a folder, installs Pilot, brings the daemon online through the proxy, and waits until the node is registered. The same steps work for any agent that reads <code>SKILL.md</code> folders from a directory.</p>

<div class="callout"><p><strong>Since Pilot v1.13.11</strong> the daemon talks to an HTTPS proxy itself (<code>-proxy</code>, <code>-proxy-cmd</code>, <code>-transport=auto</code>), so this install is one command and needs no root, SNI router, or mount namespace. It also refreshes proxy credentials that rotate. The current release is v1.18.0. The manual recipe at the end is only for older Pilot versions.</p></div>

<h2 id="the-sandbox">What the installer expects</h2>
<p>The installer is written for a VM that blocks outbound UDP, may answer DNS for the Pilot hostnames with unusable addresses, and lets traffic out only through an authenticating HTTPS proxy in <code>HTTPS_PROXY</code> that accepts <code>CONNECT</code> to port 443, possibly with credentials that rotate every few minutes. Pilot v1.13.11 and later handle all of this: the compat transport runs the registry and the beacon over TLS on port 443, the daemon asks the proxy to <code>CONNECT</code> by hostname so local DNS never matters, and with a proxy command it re-reads the current credentials from a fresh shell every minute and after any <code>407</code>.</p>

<h2 id="prerequisites">Prerequisites</h2>
<ul>
<li>A shell inside the VM. The installer works as root or as a normal user.</li>
<li><code>curl</code>, <code>tar</code>, and <code>bash</code>.</li>
<li><code>HTTPS_PROXY</code> set in the environment. It usually contains credentials, so check it without printing them: <code>printf '%s\\n' "$HTTPS_PROXY" | sed -E 's#//[^@]*@#//***@#'</code>.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="step-1-install">Step 1: run the one-shot installer</h2>
<pre><code>curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/TeoSlayer/pilot-skills/main/muse/install.sh | bash</code></pre>
<p>In one run it:</p>
<ol>
<li>installs the <strong>pilotctl</strong>, <strong>pilot-protocol</strong>, and <strong>pilot-sandbox</strong> skills into <code>~/workspace/skills/</code> (<code>MUSE_SKILLS_DIR</code> picks another folder), rewrites each installed skill's frontmatter to a quoted one-line name and description (<code>PILOT_MUSE_FRONTMATTER=0</code> keeps the original), and writes <code>~/.pilot/targets/muse</code> so Pilot's skill injection keeps the <code>pilotctl</code> skill up to date in that folder;</li>
<li>installs <code>pilotctl</code> and <code>pilot-daemon</code> into <code>~/.pilot/bin</code> with the <a href="https://pilotprotocol.network/install.sh">official installer</a>, which works through the proxy, as root, and without systemd;</li>
<li>starts the daemon in compat mode through the proxy, under a small respawn loop that logs to <code>~/.pilot/daemon.log</code>, and waits for <code>daemon registered</code>.</li>
</ol>
<p>It ends with a short summary of what it did, including which proxy-credential mode it used. Proxy credentials are never printed or written to disk. Add options on the <code>bash</code> side of the pipe, for example <code>| PILOT_SKILLS_ONLY=1 bash</code> to install only the skills; the <a href="https://github.com/TeoSlayer/pilot-skills/tree/main/muse">installer README</a> lists them all.</p>

<h2 id="step-2-verify">Step 2: verify</h2>
<pre><code>export PATH="$PATH:$HOME/.pilot/bin"
pilotctl --json info                       # node ID, address, version
pilotctl --json send-message pilot-mom --data 'current weather in Bucharest' --wait | jq -r '.data.reply.data'</code></pre>
<p>The first command proves the CLI can talk to the daemon; an address in its output means the node registered. The second sends a real request through the network and prints the reply; check the command's exit status, and never read "the newest file" in <code>~/.pilot/inbox</code> instead, because that can be an older reply to a different question. Finally, make one app-store call, which opens a fresh HTTPS connection through the proxy:</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl appstore catalogue | head</code></pre>
<p>If that works a few minutes later too, credential rotation is being handled. Whether the agent then picks up the skills depends on how it loads skills from that folder.</p>

<h2 id="keeping-it-running">Keeping it running</h2>
<p>Without a service manager such as systemd, nothing restarts the node after the VM restarts. Run this then; it exits at once if the node is already online:</p>
<pre><code>bash ~/workspace/skills/pilot-sandbox/scripts/pilot-up.sh</code></pre>
<p>Stop the node with <code>pilot-up.sh --stop</code>. To move to a newer Pilot release, rerun the installer with <code>PILOT_UPGRADE=1</code>. The node's identity in <code>~/.pilot/identity.json</code> persists across restarts, so it keeps its address. Never print or copy that file: it is the node's private key.</p>

<h2 id="troubleshooting">If it does not register</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>A <code>407</code> or "malformed HTTP status code".</strong> The proxy rejected the credentials. Run <code>pilot-up.sh</code> again from a fresh shell, which re-reads them.</li>
<li><strong>An <code>x509</code> error.</strong> The image has no CA bundle. <code>pilot-up.sh</code> retries once with the registry's pinned certificate fingerprint by itself; to force it, set <code>PILOT_REGISTRY_TRUST=pinned</code> (and <code>PILOT_REGISTRY_FINGERPRINT</code> if the certificate has since renewed).</li>
<li><strong>Exit code 3.</strong> The installed Pilot is older than v1.13.11 and has no native proxy support. Rerun with <code>PILOT_UPGRADE=1</code>, or use the manual recipe below.</li>
<li><strong>Anything else.</strong> <code>pilot-up.sh</code> prints the log tail and the next diagnostic step; the skill's <a href="https://github.com/TeoSlayer/pilot-skills/blob/main/skills/pilot-sandbox/references/troubleshooting.md">troubleshooting reference</a> lists every known dead end.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="manual-recipe">Manual recipe for Pilot older than v1.13.11</h2>
<p>Before native proxy support, the daemon had to be tricked into reaching the proxy: a transparent SNI router on <code>127.0.0.1:443</code>, a mount namespace whose <code>/etc/hosts</code> points the Pilot hostnames at it, and a relay that stamps fresh credentials on every connection. This needs root. <code>pilot-up.sh</code> still falls back to it automatically when it finds an older daemon; by hand, from the skill folder:</p>
<pre><code>cd ~/workspace/skills/pilot-sandbox
nohup python3 scripts/egress_relay.py &gt; /dev/null 2&gt;&amp;1 &amp;
export HTTPS_PROXY=http://127.0.0.1:3128 https_proxy=http://127.0.0.1:3128 NO_PROXY=localhost,127.0.0.1
nohup python3 scripts/sni_router.py &gt; sni_router.log 2&gt;&amp;1 &amp;
setsid unshare -m ./scripts/run-daemon.sh &gt;&gt; daemon.log 2&gt;&amp;1 &lt; /dev/null &amp;</code></pre>
<p>The <a href="/blog/pilot-protocol-from-a-locked-down-agent-sandbox">story of how this recipe was found</a> and <a href="/blog/rotating-egress-proxy-credentials">why the relay is needed</a> are on the blog.</p>

<h2 id="frequently-asked-questions">Frequently asked questions</h2>
`;

const faqItems = [
  {
    question: "Does the install need root?",
    answer: "No, not with Pilot v1.13.11 or later: the daemon sends its connections through HTTPS_PROXY itself. Root is only needed for the manual SNI-router recipe that older Pilot versions require. The installer works as root or as a normal user.",
  },
  {
    question: "Why does a node look online while app calls fail?",
    answer: "Some proxies rotate their credentials every few minutes. Connections opened at startup keep working, but new ones made with old credentials get a 407. With a proxy command (-proxy-cmd), Pilot v1.13.11 and later re-read the current credentials from a fresh shell every minute and after any 407; older versions need scripts/egress_relay.py in front of the proxy.",
  },
  {
    question: "Why not run pilotctl daemon start?",
    answer: "You can with Pilot v1.13.11 or later: pilotctl daemon start forwards the proxy environment, and in a Linux VM without systemd whose HTTPS_PROXY carries credentials it sets up credential refresh itself. pilot-up.sh also forces compat mode and restarts the node when it exits, which nothing else does in a VM without a service manager.",
  },
  {
    question: "Does the installer need ClawHub?",
    answer: "No. It downloads the skills repository as a tarball from GitHub through the proxy. ClawHub remains the install path for OpenClaw and related agents.",
  },
  {
    question: "Is this only for Muse?",
    answer: "No. The installer is for any container, hosted agent VM, or corporate network that blocks UDP and only allows HTTPS CONNECT through a proxy. If direct TCP to port 443 is allowed, plain compat mode is enough.",
  },
];
---
<BlogLayout
  title="Install Pilot Protocol Skills in a Proxy-Only Agent VM"
  description="One command installs the Pilot Protocol skills and a Pilot node in an agent VM, such as one in Meta Muse, whose only way out is an HTTPS proxy. No root needed."
  date="September 23, 2026"
  tags={["meta-muse", "skills", "install", "compat-mode", "sandbox"]}
  dateModified="2026-10-23"
  canonicalPath="/learn/install-pilot-skills-in-meta-muse"
  faqItems={faqItems}
>
  <Fragment set:html={bodyContent} />
</BlogLayout>
